🗣️ Communication Practice — 100–200 Words

Topic: How I Approach a Problem

When I face a technical problem, I first try to understand what is actually being asked instead of immediately writing code. I break the problem into smaller parts and identify the inputs, outputs, constraints, and possible edge cases. Then I think about a simple solution before looking for an optimized approach. Once I have an approach, I test it with a small example manually. This helps me identify logical mistakes early. After that, I implement the solution and test it with different cases. If the solution fails, I analyze the reason instead of randomly changing the code. I believe this systematic approach is important for software engineers because real-world development involves understanding problems, debugging, optimizing, and communicating solutions—not just writing code.

🎤 Speaking task:

Explain how you solve a programming problem in 60 seconds.

Use:

Understand → Break down → Approach → Implement → Test → Optimize


